-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Supervising Unseen Pixels:** Supervising rendering loss for pixels not visible in context views (obscured or outside frustums) is counterproductive and can be destructive to the model’s performance. This is addressed by the loss masking strategy [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Local Minima:** Original 3D-GS uses iterative optimization which can get stuck in local minima. Splatt3R avoids this by using a pre-trained foundation model (MASt3R) and direct supervision of positions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Uncalibrated Input:** Unlike pixelSplat which uses camera intrinsics $K$ to compute location $\mu = K^{-1}ud + \Delta$, Splatt3R directly supervises positions using ground-truth point clouds from uncalibrated images [S1, unofficial].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is a single forward pass, achieving 4 FPS at $512 \times 512$ resolution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On ScanNet++ test scenes, Splatt3R achieves PSNR 19.66, SSIM 0.757, LPIPS 0.234 for Close view overlap ($\phi=0.9, \psi=0.9$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** The method is designed for generalization to uncalibrated, in-the-wild images [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Pose-free, feed-forward, handles uncalibrated images, supports extrapolation beyond stereo baseline.
-*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades with wider baselines (lower overlap). Relies on the quality of the MASt3R backbone.
-*   **When to use:** When camera parameters are unknown and per-scene optimization is too slow.
-*   **When not to use:** When high precision is required for very wide baselines or when camera intrinsics are known and can be leveraged for better accuracy.
+
+### Time and Memory Complexity
+*   **Time:** Splatt3R runs at 4 FPS for 512x512 resolution [S1, unofficial]. This is significantly faster than iterative per-scene optimization methods like standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Memory usage is dominated by the ViT encoder and the Gaussian head. The method predicts per-pixel Gaussians, resulting in $H \times W$ primitives per image [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Measured Performance
+On the ScanNet++ test scenes with Close view overlap ($\phi=0.9, \psi=0.9$):
+*   PSNR: 19.66 dB
+*   SSIM: 0.757
+*   LPIPS: 0.234 [S1, unofficial]
+
+### Strengths and Limitations
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Pose-free: Does not require camera intrinsics or extrinsics [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Generalizable: Trained on ScanNet++, it generalizes to uncalibrated, in-the-wild images [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Extrapolation: Can synthesize views beyond the stereo baseline, unlike many siblings [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Performance degrades with wider baselines (lower $\phi, \psi$) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Relies on the quality of the MASt3R point cloud prediction; errors in geometry propagate to Gaussian parameters [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Constant color parameterization may limit appearance quality compared to view-dependent SH [S1, unofficial].
+
+### When to Use
+*   Use Splatt3R when you have a pair of uncalibrated images and need fast, generalizable novel view synthesis without camera calibration [S1, unofficial].
+*   Use when extrapolation to viewpoints outside the input stereo pair is required [S1, unofficial].
+
+### When Not to Use
+*   Do not use if high-precision camera poses are available and iterative optimization is acceptable, as standard 3D-GS will likely yield higher quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   Do not use if view-dependent appearance is critical and the constant color ablation is insufficient [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Splatt3R was introduced in 2024 by Brandon, Zheng, Iro, and Victor from the University of Oxford [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the MASt3R architecture, extending it to predict 3D Gaussian Splats for novel view synthesis. The method was trained on the ScanNet++ dataset [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of existing generalizable 3D-GS methods that only supervise novel viewpoints between input stereo views, by introducing a loss masking strategy to support extrapolation [S1, unofficial].
+Splatt3R was introduced in 2024 by Brandon, Zheng, Iro, and Victor from the University of Oxford [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the MASt3R architecture, which is a foundation model for 3D geometry reconstruction from uncalibrated stereo pairs [S1, unofficial]. The method was trained on the ScanNet++ dataset [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of existing generalizable 3D-GS methods that only supervise interpolated viewpoints, by introducing a loss masking strategy to enable extrapolation [S1, unofficial].
 
